    write(output_unit, '(a)') 'optional true'
    write(output_unit, '(a)') 'longname second anisotropy rotation angle (degrees)'
    write(output_unit, '(a)', advance='no') 'description is a rotation angle of the hydraulic conductivity tensor in degrees. The a'
    write(output_unit, '(a)', advance='no') 'ngle represents the second of three sequential rotations of the hydraulic conductivity'
    write(output_unit, '(a)', advance='no') ' ellipsoid. Following the rotation by ANGLE1 described above, ANGLE2 rotates the ellip'
    write(output_unit, '(a)', advance='no') 'soid about its K22 axis (out of the x - y plane). An array can be specified for ANGLE2'
    write(output_unit, '(a)', advance='no') ' only if ANGLE1 is also specified. A positive value of ANGLE2 represents clockwise rot'
    write(output_unit, '(a)', advance='no') 'ation when viewed from any point on the positive K22 axis, looking toward the center o'
    write(output_unit, '(a)', advance='no') 'f the ellipsoid. A value of zero indicates that the K11 axis lies within the x - y pla'
    write(output_unit, '(a)', advance='no') 'ne. If ANGLE2 is not specified, default values of zero are assigned to ANGLE2 and ANGL'
    write(output_unit, '(a)', advance='no') 'E3; connections that are not user-designated as vertical are assumed to be strictly ho'
    write(output_unit, '(a)', advance='no') 'rizontal (that is, to have no z component to their orientation); and connection length'
    write(output_unit, '(a)', advance='no') 's are based on horizontal distances.'
