    write(output_unit, '(a)', advance='no') 'description Apply mass source loading rate to specified CELLID or highest underlying c'
    write(output_unit, '(a)', advance='no') 'ell with a cell saturation greater than zero. The highest\_saturated option has an add'
    write(output_unit, '(a)', advance='no') 'itional complication for certain types of grids specified using the DISU Package. When'
    write(output_unit, '(a)', advance='no') ' the DISU Package is used, a cell may have more than one cell underlying it. If the ov'
    write(output_unit, '(a)', advance='no') 'erlying cell were to become inactive, there is no straightforward method for determini'
    write(output_unit, '(a)', advance='no') 'ng how to apportion the mass source loading rate to the underlying cells. In this case'
    write(output_unit, '(a)', advance='no') ', the approach described by \cite{modflowusg} is used. The mass source loading rate is'
    write(output_unit, '(a)', advance='no') ' assigned to the first active cell encountered (determined by searching through the un'
    write(output_unit, '(a)', advance='no') 'derlying cell numbers from the lowest number to the highest number). In this manner, t'
    write(output_unit, '(a)', advance='no') 'he total mass source loading rate is conserved; however, the spatial distribution of t'
    write(output_unit, '(a)', advance='no') 'he applied mass source loading rate may not be maintained as layers become dry or wet '
    write(output_unit, '(a)', advance='no') 'during a simulation.'
