    write(output_unit, '(a)') 'longname under relaxation scheme'
    write(output_unit, '(a)', advance='no') 'description is an optional keyword that defines the nonlinear under-relaxation schemes'
    write(output_unit, '(a)', advance='no') ' used. Under-relaxation is also known as dampening, and is used to reduce the size of '
    write(output_unit, '(a)', advance='no') 'the calculated dependent variable before proceeding to the next outer iteration.  Unde'
    write(output_unit, '(a)', advance='no') 'r-relaxation can be an effective tool for highly nonlinear models when there are large'
    write(output_unit, '(a)', advance='no') ' and often counteracting changes in the calculated dependent variable between successi'
    write(output_unit, '(a)', advance='no') 've outer iterations.  By default under-relaxation is not used.  NONE - under-relaxatio'
    write(output_unit, '(a)', advance='no') 'n is not used (default). SIMPLE - Simple under-relaxation scheme with a fixed relaxati'
    write(output_unit, '(a)', advance='no') 'on factor (UNDER\_RELAXATION\_GAMMA) is used.  COOLEY - Cooley under-relaxation scheme'
    write(output_unit, '(a)', advance='no') ' is used.  DBD - delta-bar-delta under-relaxation is used.  Note that the under-relaxa'
    write(output_unit, '(a)', advance='no') 'tion schemes are often used in conjunction with problems that use the Newton-Raphson f'
    write(output_unit, '(a)', advance='no') 'ormulation, however, experience has indicated that they also work well for non-Newton '
    write(output_unit, '(a)', advance='no') 'problems, such as those with the wet/dry options of MODFLOW 6.'
    write(output_unit, '(a)') ''
    write(output_unit, '(a)') ''
    write(output_unit, '(a)') 'block nonlinear'
    write(output_unit, '(a)') 'name under_relaxation_gamma'
    write(output_unit, '(a)') 'type double precision'
    write(output_unit, '(a)') 'reader urword'
    write(output_unit, '(a)') 'optional true'
    write(output_unit, '(a)', advance='no') 'longname relaxation factor for SIMPLE or the history or memory term factor for the Coo'
    write(output_unit, '(a)', advance='no') 'ley and delta-bar-delta algorithms'
    write(output_unit, '(a)') ''
    write(output_unit, '(a)', advance='no') 'description real value defining either the relaxation factor for the SIMPLE scheme or '
    write(output_unit, '(a)', advance='no') 'the history or memory term factor of the Cooley and delta-bar-delta algorithms. For th'
    write(output_unit, '(a)', advance='no') 'e SIMPLE scheme, a value of one indicates that there is no under-relaxation and the fu'
    write(output_unit, '(a)', advance='no') 'll head change is applied.  This value can be gradually reduced from one as a way to i'
    write(output_unit, '(a)', advance='no') 'mprove convergence; for well behaved problems, using a value less than one can increas'
    write(output_unit, '(a)', advance='no') 'e the number of outer iterations required for convergence and needlessly increase run '
    write(output_unit, '(a)', advance='no') 'times.  UNDER\_RELAXATION\_GAMMA must be greater than zero for the SIMPLE scheme or th'
    write(output_unit, '(a)', advance='no') 'e program will terminate with an error.  For the Cooley and delta-bar-delta schemes, U'
    write(output_unit, '(a)', advance='no') 'NDER\_RELAXATION\_GAMMA is a memory term that can range between zero and one. When UND'
    write(output_unit, '(a)', advance='no') 'ER\_RELAXATION\_GAMMA is zero, only the most recent history (previous iteration value)'
    write(output_unit, '(a)', advance='no') ' is maintained. As UNDER\_RELAXATION\_GAMMA is increased, past history of iteration ch'
    write(output_unit, '(a)', advance='no') 'anges has greater influence on the memory term. The memory term is maintained as an ex'
    write(output_unit, '(a)', advance='no') 'ponential average of past changes. Retaining some past history can overcome granular b'
    write(output_unit, '(a)', advance='no') 'ehavior in the calculated function surface and therefore helps to overcome cyclic patt'
    write(output_unit, '(a)', advance='no') 'erns of non-convergence. The value usually ranges from 0.1 to 0.3; a value of 0.2 work'
    write(output_unit, '(a)', advance='no') 's well for most problems. UNDER\_RELAXATION\_GAMMA only needs to be specified if UNDER'
    write(output_unit, '(a)', advance='no') '\_RELAXATION is not NONE.'
    write(output_unit, '(a)') ''
    write(output_unit, '(a)') ''
    write(output_unit, '(a)') 'block nonlinear'
    write(output_unit, '(a)') 'name under_relaxation_theta'
    write(output_unit, '(a)') 'type double precision'
    write(output_unit, '(a)') 'reader urword'
    write(output_unit, '(a)') 'optional true'
    write(output_unit, '(a)') 'longname under relaxation reduction factor'
    write(output_unit, '(a)', advance='no') 'description real value defining the reduction factor for the learning rate (under-rela'
    write(output_unit, '(a)', advance='no') 'xation term) of the delta-bar-delta algorithm. The value of UNDER\_RELAXATION\_THETA i'
    write(output_unit, '(a)', advance='no') 's between zero and one. If the change in the dependent-variable (for example, head) is'
    write(output_unit, '(a)', advance='no') ' of opposite sign to that of the previous iteration, the under-relaxation term is redu'
    write(output_unit, '(a)', advance='no') 'ced by a factor of UNDER\_RELAXATION\_THETA. The value usually ranges from 0.3 to 0.9;'
    write(output_unit, '(a)', advance='no') ' a value of 0.7 works well for most problems. UNDER\_RELAXATION\_THETA only needs to b'
    write(output_unit, '(a)', advance='no') 'e specified if UNDER\_RELAXATION is DBD.'
    write(output_unit, '(a)') ''
    write(output_unit, '(a)') ''
    write(output_unit, '(a)') 'block nonlinear'
    write(output_unit, '(a)') 'name under_relaxation_kappa'
    write(output_unit, '(a)') 'type double precision'
    write(output_unit, '(a)') 'reader urword'
    write(output_unit, '(a)') 'optional true'
    write(output_unit, '(a)') 'longname under relaxation increment for the learning rate'
    write(output_unit, '(a)', advance='no') 'description real value defining the increment for the learning rate (under-relaxation '
    write(output_unit, '(a)', advance='no') 'term) of the delta-bar-delta algorithm. The value of UNDER\_RELAXATION\_kappa is betwe'
    write(output_unit, '(a)', advance='no') 'en zero and one. If the change in the dependent-variable (for example, head) is of the'
    write(output_unit, '(a)', advance='no') ' same sign to that of the previous iteration, the under-relaxation term is increased b'
    write(output_unit, '(a)', advance='no') 'y an increment of UNDER\_RELAXATION\_KAPPA. The value usually ranges from 0.03 to 0.3;'
    write(output_unit, '(a)', advance='no') ' a value of 0.1 works well for most problems. UNDER\_RELAXATION\_KAPPA only needs to b'
    write(output_unit, '(a)', advance='no') 'e specified if UNDER\_RELAXATION is DBD.'
    write(output_unit, '(a)') ''
    write(output_unit, '(a)') ''
    write(output_unit, '(a)') 'block nonlinear'
    write(output_unit, '(a)') 'name under_relaxation_momentum'
    write(output_unit, '(a)') 'type double precision'
    write(output_unit, '(a)') 'reader urword'
    write(output_unit, '(a)') 'optional true'
    write(output_unit, '(a)') 'longname fraction of past history changes that is added as a momentum term'
    write(output_unit, '(a)', advance='no') 'description real value defining the fraction of past history changes that is added as '
    write(output_unit, '(a)', advance='no') 'a momentum term to the step change for a nonlinear iteration. The value of UNDER\_RELA'
    write(output_unit, '(a)', advance='no') 'XATION\_MOMENTUM is between zero and one. A large momentum term should only be used wh'
    write(output_unit, '(a)', advance='no') 'en small learning rates are expected. Small amounts of the momentum term help converge'
    write(output_unit, '(a)', advance='no') 'nce. The value usually ranges from 0.0001 to 0.1; a value of 0.001 works well for most'
    write(output_unit, '(a)', advance='no') ' problems. UNDER\_RELAXATION\_MOMENTUM only needs to be specified if UNDER\_RELAXATION'
    write(output_unit, '(a)', advance='no') ' is DBD.'
    write(output_unit, '(a)') ''
    write(output_unit, '(a)') ''
    write(output_unit, '(a)') 'block nonlinear'
    write(output_unit, '(a)') 'name backtracking_number'
    write(output_unit, '(a)') 'type integer'
    write(output_unit, '(a)') 'reader urword'
    write(output_unit, '(a)') 'optional true'
    write(output_unit, '(a)') 'longname maximum number of backtracking iterations'
    write(output_unit, '(a)', advance='no') 'description integer value defining the maximum number of backtracking iterations allow'
    write(output_unit, '(a)', advance='no') 'ed for residual reduction computations. If BACKTRACKING\_NUMBER = 0 then the backtrack'
    write(output_unit, '(a)', advance='no') 'ing iterations are omitted. The value usually ranges from 2 to 20; a value of 10 works'
    write(output_unit, '(a)', advance='no') ' well for most problems.'
    write(output_unit, '(a)') ''
    write(output_unit, '(a)') ''
    write(output_unit, '(a)') 'block nonlinear'
    write(output_unit, '(a)') 'name backtracking_tolerance'
    write(output_unit, '(a)') 'type double precision'
    write(output_unit, '(a)') 'reader urword'
    write(output_unit, '(a)') 'optional true'
    write(output_unit, '(a)') 'longname tolerance for residual change that is allowed for residual reduction'
    write(output_unit, '(a)', advance='no') 'description real value defining the tolerance for residual change that is allowed for '
    write(output_unit, '(a)', advance='no') 'residual reduction computations. BACKTRACKING\_TOLERANCE should not be less than one t'
    write(output_unit, '(a)', advance='no') 'o avoid getting stuck in local minima. A large value serves to check for extreme resid'
    write(output_unit, '(a)', advance='no') 'ual increases, while a low value serves to control step size more severely. The value '
    write(output_unit, '(a)', advance='no') 'usually ranges from 1.0 to 10$^6$; a value of 10$^4$ works well for most problems but '
    write(output_unit, '(a)', advance='no') 'lower values like 1.1 may be required for harder problems. BACKTRACKING\_TOLERANCE onl'
    write(output_unit, '(a)', advance='no') 'y needs to be specified if BACKTRACKING\_NUMBER is greater than zero.'
    write(output_unit, '(a)') ''
    write(output_unit, '(a)') ''
    write(output_unit, '(a)') 'block nonlinear'
    write(output_unit, '(a)') 'name backtracking_reduction_factor'
    write(output_unit, '(a)') 'type double precision'
    write(output_unit, '(a)') 'reader urword'
    write(output_unit, '(a)') 'optional true'
    write(output_unit, '(a)') 'longname reduction in step size used for residual reduction computations'
    write(output_unit, '(a)', advance='no') 'description real value defining the reduction in step size used for residual reduction'
    write(output_unit, '(a)', advance='no') ' computations. The value of BACKTRACKING\_REDUCTION\_FACTOR is between zero and one. T'
    write(output_unit, '(a)', advance='no') 'he value usually ranges from 0.1 to 0.3; a value of 0.2 works well for most problems. '
    write(output_unit, '(a)', advance='no') 'BACKTRACKING\_REDUCTION\_FACTOR only needs to be specified if BACKTRACKING\_NUMBER is '
    write(output_unit, '(a)', advance='no') 'greater than zero.'
    write(output_unit, '(a)') ''
    write(output_unit, '(a)') ''
    write(output_unit, '(a)') 'block nonlinear'
    write(output_unit, '(a)') 'name backtracking_residual_limit'
    write(output_unit, '(a)') 'type double precision'
    write(output_unit, '(a)') 'reader urword'
    write(output_unit, '(a)') 'optional true'
    write(output_unit, '(a)') 'longname limit to which the residual is reduced with backtracking'
    write(output_unit, '(a)', advance='no') 'description real value defining the limit to which the residual is reduced with backtr'
    write(output_unit, '(a)', advance='no') 'acking. If the residual is smaller than BACKTRACKING\_RESIDUAL\_LIMIT, then further ba'
    write(output_unit, '(a)', advance='no') 'cktracking is not performed. A value of 100 is suitable for large problems and residua'
    write(output_unit, '(a)', advance='no') 'l reduction to smaller values may only slow down computations. BACKTRACKING\_RESIDUAL\'
    write(output_unit, '(a)', advance='no') '_LIMIT only needs to be specified if BACKTRACKING\_NUMBER is greater than zero.'
    write(output_unit, '(a)') ''
    write(output_unit, '(a)') ''
    write(output_unit, '(a)') ''
    write(output_unit, '(a)') '# --------------------- sln ims linear ---------------------'
    write(output_unit, '(a)') ''
    write(output_unit, '(a)') 'block linear'
    write(output_unit, '(a)') 'name inner_maximum'
    write(output_unit, '(a)') 'type integer'
    write(output_unit, '(a)') 'reader urword'
    write(output_unit, '(a)') 'optional false'
    write(output_unit, '(a)') 'longname maximum number of inner iterations'
    write(output_unit, '(a)', advance='no') 'description integer value defining the maximum number of inner (linear) iterations. Th'
    write(output_unit, '(a)', advance='no') 'e number typically depends on the characteristics of the matrix solution scheme being '
    write(output_unit, '(a)', advance='no') 'used. For nonlinear problems, INNER\_MAXIMUM usually ranges from 60 to 600; a value of'
    write(output_unit, '(a)', advance='no') ' 100 will be sufficient for most linear problems.'
    write(output_unit, '(a)') ''
    write(output_unit, '(a)') ''
    write(output_unit, '(a)') 'block linear'
    write(output_unit, '(a)') 'name inner_hclose'
    write(output_unit, '(a)') 'type double precision'
    write(output_unit, '(a)') 'reader urword'
    write(output_unit, '(a)') 'optional true'
    write(output_unit, '(a)') 'deprecated 6.1.1'
    write(output_unit, '(a)') 'longname head change tolerance'
    write(output_unit, '(a)', advance='no') 'description real value defining the head change criterion for convergence of the inner'
    write(output_unit, '(a)', advance='no') ' (linear) iterations, in units of length. When the maximum absolute value of the head '
    write(output_unit, '(a)', advance='no') 'change at all nodes during an iteration is less than or equal to INNER\_HCLOSE, the ma'
    write(output_unit, '(a)', advance='no') 'trix solver assumes convergence. Commonly, INNER\_HCLOSE is set equal to or an order o'
    write(output_unit, '(a)', advance='no') 'f magnitude less than the OUTER\_HCLOSE value specified for the NONLINEAR block.  The '
    write(output_unit, '(a)', advance='no') 'INNER\_HCLOSE keyword has been deprecated in favor of the more general INNER\_DVCLOSE '
    write(output_unit, '(a)', advance='no') '(for dependent variable), however either one can be specified in order to maintain bac'
    write(output_unit, '(a)', advance='no') 'kward compatibility.'
    write(output_unit, '(a)') ''
    write(output_unit, '(a)') ''
    write(output_unit, '(a)') 'block linear'
    write(output_unit, '(a)') 'name inner_dvclose'
    write(output_unit, '(a)') 'type double precision'
    write(output_unit, '(a)') 'reader urword'
    write(output_unit, '(a)') 'optional false'
    write(output_unit, '(a)') 'longname dependent-variable change tolerance'
    write(output_unit, '(a)', advance='no') 'description real value defining the dependent-variable (for example, head) change crit'
    write(output_unit, '(a)', advance='no') 'erion for convergence of the inner (linear) iterations, in units of the dependent-vari'
    write(output_unit, '(a)', advance='no') 'able (for example, length for head). When the maximum absolute value of the dependent-'
    write(output_unit, '(a)', advance='no') 'variable change at all nodes during an iteration is less than or equal to INNER\_DVCLO'
    write(output_unit, '(a)', advance='no') 'SE, the matrix solver assumes convergence. Commonly, INNER\_DVCLOSE is set equal to or'
    write(output_unit, '(a)', advance='no') ' an order of magnitude less than the OUTER\_DVCLOSE value specified for the NONLINEAR '
    write(output_unit, '(a)', advance='no') 'block. The keyword, INNER\_HCLOSE can be still be specified instead of INNER\_DVCLOSE '
    write(output_unit, '(a)', advance='no') 'for backward compatibility with previous versions of MODFLOW 6 but eventually INNER\_H'
    write(output_unit, '(a)', advance='no') 'CLOSE will be deprecated and specification of INNER\_HCLOSE will cause MODFLOW 6 to te'
    write(output_unit, '(a)', advance='no') 'rminate with an error.'
    write(output_unit, '(a)') ''
    write(output_unit, '(a)') ''
    write(output_unit, '(a)') 'block linear'
    write(output_unit, '(a)') 'name rcloserecord'
    write(output_unit, '(a)') 'type record inner_rclose rclose_option'
    write(output_unit, '(a)') 'reader urword'
    write(output_unit, '(a)') 'optional false'
    write(output_unit, '(a)') 'longname rclose record'
    write(output_unit, '(a)') 'description'
    write(output_unit, '(a)') ''
    write(output_unit, '(a)') 'block linear'
    write(output_unit, '(a)') 'name inner_rclose'
    write(output_unit, '(a)') 'type double precision'
    write(output_unit, '(a)') 'in_record true'
    write(output_unit, '(a)') 'reader urword'
    write(output_unit, '(a)') 'tagged true'
    write(output_unit, '(a)') 'optional false'
    write(output_unit, '(a)') 'longname flow residual tolerance'
    write(output_unit, '(a)', advance='no') 'description real value that defines the flow residual tolerance for convergence of the'
    write(output_unit, '(a)', advance='no') ' IMS linear solver and specific flow residual criteria used. This value represents the'
    write(output_unit, '(a)', advance='no') ' maximum allowable residual at any single node.  Value is in units of length cubed per'
    write(output_unit, '(a)', advance='no') ' time, and must be consistent with \mf length and time units. Usually a value of $1.0 '
    write(output_unit, '(a)', advance='no') '\times 10^{-1}$ is sufficient for the flow-residual criteria when meters and seconds a'
    write(output_unit, '(a)', advance='no') 're the defined \mf length and time.'
    write(output_unit, '(a)') ''
    write(output_unit, '(a)') ''
    write(output_unit, '(a)') 'block linear'
    write(output_unit, '(a)') 'name rclose_option'
    write(output_unit, '(a)') 'type string'
    write(output_unit, '(a)') 'tagged false'
    write(output_unit, '(a)') 'in_record true'
    write(output_unit, '(a)') 'reader urword'
    write(output_unit, '(a)') 'optional true'
    write(output_unit, '(a)') 'longname flow residual tolerance'
    write(output_unit, '(a)', advance='no') 'description an optional keyword that defines the specific flow residual criterion used'
    write(output_unit, '(a)', advance='no') '.  STRICT--an optional keyword that is used to specify that INNER\_RCLOSE represents a'
    write(output_unit, '(a)', advance='no') ' infinity-Norm (absolute convergence criteria) and that the dependent-variable (for ex'
    write(output_unit, '(a)', advance='no') 'ample, head) and flow convergence criteria must be met on the first inner iteration (t'
    write(output_unit, '(a)', advance='no') 'his criteria is equivalent to the criteria used by the MODFLOW-2005 PCG package~\citep'
    write(output_unit, '(a)', advance='no') '{hill1990preconditioned}). L2NORM\_RCLOSE--an optional keyword that is used to specify'
    write(output_unit, '(a)', advance='no') ' that INNER\_RCLOSE represents a L-2 Norm closure criteria instead of a infinity-Norm '
    write(output_unit, '(a)', advance='no') '(absolute convergence criteria). When L2NORM\_RCLOSE is specified, a reasonable initia'
    write(output_unit, '(a)', advance='no') 'l INNER\_RCLOSE value is 0.1 times the number of active cells when meters and seconds '
    write(output_unit, '(a)', advance='no') 'are the defined \mf length and time.  RELATIVE\_RCLOSE--an optional keyword that is us'
    write(output_unit, '(a)', advance='no') 'ed to specify that INNER\_RCLOSE represents a relative L-2 Norm reduction closure crit'
    write(output_unit, '(a)', advance='no') 'eria instead of a infinity-Norm (absolute convergence criteria). When RELATIVE\_RCLOSE'
    write(output_unit, '(a)', advance='no') ' is specified, a reasonable initial INNER\_RCLOSE value is $1.0 \times 10^{-4}$ and co'
    write(output_unit, '(a)', advance='no') 'nvergence is achieved for a given inner (linear) iteration when $\Delta h \le$ INNER\_'
    write(output_unit, '(a)', advance='no') 'DVCLOSE and the current L-2 Norm is $\le$ the product of the RELATIVE\_RCLOSE and the '
    write(output_unit, '(a)', advance='no') 'initial L-2 Norm for the current inner (linear) iteration. If RCLOSE\_OPTION is not sp'
    write(output_unit, '(a)', advance='no') 'ecified, an absolute residual (infinity-norm) criterion is used.'
    write(output_unit, '(a)') ''
    write(output_unit, '(a)') ''
    write(output_unit, '(a)') 'block linear'
    write(output_unit, '(a)') 'name linear_acceleration'
    write(output_unit, '(a)') 'type string'
    write(output_unit, '(a)') 'reader urword'
    write(output_unit, '(a)') 'optional false'
    write(output_unit, '(a)') 'longname linear acceleration method'
    write(output_unit, '(a)', advance='no') 'description a keyword that defines the linear acceleration method used by the default '
    write(output_unit, '(a)', advance='no') 'IMS linear solvers.  CG - preconditioned conjugate gradient method.  BICGSTAB - precon'
    write(output_unit, '(a)', advance='no') 'ditioned bi-conjugate gradient stabilized method.'
    write(output_unit, '(a)') ''
    write(output_unit, '(a)') ''
    write(output_unit, '(a)') 'block linear'
    write(output_unit, '(a)') 'name relaxation_factor'
    write(output_unit, '(a)') 'type double precision'
    write(output_unit, '(a)') 'reader urword'
    write(output_unit, '(a)') 'optional true'
    write(output_unit, '(a)') 'longname relaxation factor used by ILU factorization'
    write(output_unit, '(a)', advance='no') 'description optional real value that defines the relaxation factor used by the incompl'
    write(output_unit, '(a)', advance='no') 'ete LU factorization preconditioners (MILU(0) and MILUT). RELAXATION\_FACTOR is unitle'
    write(output_unit, '(a)', advance='no') 'ss and should be greater than or equal to 0.0 and less than or equal to 1.0. RELAXATIO'
    write(output_unit, '(a)', advance='no') 'N\_FACTOR values of about 1.0 are commonly used, and experience suggests that converge'
    write(output_unit, '(a)', advance='no') 'nce can be optimized in some cases with relax values of 0.97. A RELAXATION\_FACTOR val'
    write(output_unit, '(a)', advance='no') 'ue of 0.0 will result in either ILU(0) or ILUT preconditioning (depending on the value'
    write(output_unit, '(a)', advance='no') ' specified for PRECONDITIONER\_LEVELS and/or PRECONDITIONER\_DROP\_TOLERANCE). By defa'
    write(output_unit, '(a)', advance='no') 'ult,  RELAXATION\_FACTOR is zero.'
    write(output_unit, '(a)') ''
    write(output_unit, '(a)') ''
    write(output_unit, '(a)') 'block linear'
    write(output_unit, '(a)') 'name preconditioner_levels'
    write(output_unit, '(a)') 'type integer'
    write(output_unit, '(a)') 'reader urword'
    write(output_unit, '(a)') 'optional true'
    write(output_unit, '(a)') 'longname level of fill for ILU decomposition'
    write(output_unit, '(a)', advance='no') 'description optional integer value defining the level of fill for ILU decomposition us'
    write(output_unit, '(a)', advance='no') 'ed in the ILUT and MILUT preconditioners. Higher levels of fill provide more robustnes'
    write(output_unit, '(a)', advance='no') 's but also require more memory. For optimal performance, it is suggested that a large '
    write(output_unit, '(a)', advance='no') 'level of fill be applied (7 or 8) with use of a drop tolerance. Specification of a PRE'
    write(output_unit, '(a)', advance='no') 'CONDITIONER\_LEVELS value greater than zero results in use of the ILUT preconditioner.'
    write(output_unit, '(a)', advance='no') ' By default, PRECONDITIONER\_LEVELS is zero and the zero-fill incomplete LU factorizat'
    write(output_unit, '(a)', advance='no') 'ion preconditioners (ILU(0) and MILU(0)) are used.'
    write(output_unit, '(a)') ''
    write(output_unit, '(a)') ''
    write(output_unit, '(a)') 'block linear'
    write(output_unit, '(a)') 'name preconditioner_drop_tolerance'
    write(output_unit, '(a)') 'type double precision'
    write(output_unit, '(a)') 'reader urword'
    write(output_unit, '(a)') 'optional true'
    write(output_unit, '(a)') 'longname drop tolerance used to drop preconditioner terms'
    write(output_unit, '(a)', advance='no') 'description optional real value that defines the drop tolerance used to drop precondit'
    write(output_unit, '(a)', advance='no') 'ioner terms based on the magnitude of matrix entries in the ILUT and MILUT preconditio'
    write(output_unit, '(a)', advance='no') 'ners. A value of $10^{-4}$ works well for most problems. By default, PRECONDITIONER\_D'
    write(output_unit, '(a)', advance='no') 'ROP\_TOLERANCE is zero and the zero-fill incomplete LU factorization preconditioners ('
